[{:db/id "q-14141/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer/id #uuid "873eaede-5120-5202-8ab0-6ff827aae300" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14141/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "cc17ab0e-4d7f-5fb4-b245-11b963aa0357" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14141/selection/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" :answer/id #uuid "adda1d6a-7132-596e-975a-6b35bfde7502" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14141/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer/id #uuid "685a3ca8-3740-5aeb-a97a-e7d1b6fa760d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14141/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "88949f27-0690-5d39-bd5b-4325a8444108" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14141/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "65bcd3d2-be94-577a-b059-0f400fb19888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-14141/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer-field/choices ["q-14141/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" "q-14141/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-14141/selection/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" "q-14141/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" "q-14141/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14141" :question/id #uuid "3bb110d9-2de4-5865-bc6c-c79c5f02f897" :question/math-academy-id "q-14141" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}+16x-16,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{16}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 16, & \\pm 8, & \\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 16.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-14141/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14141"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-14145"] :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-83654/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "ab96008f-9b5f-51c8-9afc-ff085673dcfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83654/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "8fc136cb-9ac3-54f1-8bda-c73a5ec5e411" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83654/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "dec4d002-13f6-5a86-a8b8-93c6e78d53f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83654/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "56c12eca-e183-58f0-b425-b8d89f27fa41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83654/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "ed0a597c-b32e-5a06-a624-0501ec5b7a15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83654/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bba5c28-2c25-5006-bd8e-c67cf9948ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83654/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-83654/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-83654/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" "q-83654/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-83654/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-83654/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83654" :question/id #uuid "02c855c8-9507-553d-b37d-de70c503e21a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83654" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+5x-3$ has a root in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm 1,\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n|  | $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(2{x}^{2}+2x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(2)(6) \\\\  & =4-48 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-83654/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83654"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112877/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "d51b0b64-14ee-52f3-8b05-aef2d9f3bc20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112877/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "0cd806d5-cac7-5ab8-a47e-d04d92de858c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112877/selection/71b1db0b2a675655ec27a2d2ceef2e86712a68eb78548232215378e0a354fb4c" :answer/id #uuid "3d044d43-04b6-5470-b760-526c2119b7d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112877/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "0e2a0c28-1458-5844-8c84-fc64358082b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112877/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "feab9798-b8b0-5866-8c2e-cfeb09826f34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112877/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0036bd73-610c-537a-a14e-f81117e7e6e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112877/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-112877/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-112877/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-112877/selection/71b1db0b2a675655ec27a2d2ceef2e86712a68eb78548232215378e0a354fb4c" "q-112877/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-112877/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112877" :question/id #uuid "0ced084c-e5cc-510a-ae57-0a31b5977172" :question/math-academy-id "q-112877" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }9}{\\text{factor of }-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112877/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112877"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-270438/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "148c24da-df45-50c7-b327-bf3cb25c2afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-270438/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b64a15-abf7-58be-9490-19f21b3eaf93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-270438/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-270438/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-270438" :question/id #uuid "eef2063a-ea31-5e8a-8994-03247a623cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-270438" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=26{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+4x+4$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 2,$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $26,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 13,$ and $\\pm 26.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{26}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{26},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-270438/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-270438"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
